            "text": "At first light, just before traffic awakens, I like to walk the quiet avenue and listen to the trees. Their dialogue is silent to ears tuned only to engines, yet once you realize it exists, a whole civic parliament reveals itself. Every wooden pillar stands like a seasoned councillor, trading gossip through the underground telephone lines of intertwined roots and whispering leaves. The plane tree at the corner reviews overnight meteorological reports with the ginkgo opposite, comparing notes on humidity, preparing stomata accordingly. Younger saplings, insecure yet eager, stretch a little farther than yesterday, challenging lamppost borders the way ambitious interns test office politics.\n\nStreet sweepers pass, displacing yesterday\u2019s confetti of petals. The oaks chuckle, remembering centuries when rivers, not asphalt, ran here. Their laughter rustles wrappers that remained invisible to me until the sound framed them as punchlines. A pigeon lands, rearranging its feathers like a hasty public speaker; the lindens pause their debate to grant the floor, an act of urban courtesy more refined than most committee meetings I have attended.\n\nSunlight finally reaches the neighborhood bakery, releasing caffeine-sweet aromas that drift like persuasive lobbyists. Humans emerge, earbuds plugged, screens glowing, stepping squarely into discussions they cannot hear. Yet the trees continue, patient stenographers of climate, recording carbon signatures, storing community memory ring by ring. When the day\u2019s chorus peaks with horns and hurry, I withdraw, carrying their minutes inside me: a small reminder that legislation of life proceeds everywhere, even without our vote, for them each dawn."
          }
